WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:05.440
I don’t even like using the word
urn to be quite honest,

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:06.720
it just sounds a bit morbid.

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:11.040
This is a piece of my grandmother
that I keep with me all the time.

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:27.160
Over the past decade or so, ashes
creations have started to appear.

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:37.680
I look at it and I just remember
all the good times we had.

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:42.800
In Classical Greece and Rome,

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:46.760
you had both burial and
cremation practices taking place.

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:50.200
Of course in other parts
of the world, traditional aspects

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:52.560
in Buddhism - again cremation.

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:57.960
Of course the Jews, never cremation,
burial normative. Islam, burial.

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:02.680
But of course, long before this
India was a cremation culture.

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:06.040
And once Christianity became
established in the UK...

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:10.920
...and that continued really
until the mid-19th Century

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:15.120
when, with industrialisation and
the rise of big towns, big cities,

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:16.960
church yards got full fast.

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:20.880
The miasmas, the gases that
were rising from putrefied bodies

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:23.120
were considered to be very unhealthy.

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:27.560
The Cremation Society was founded
in 1874 by Sir Henry Thompson

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:29.120
who was a celebrated surgeon.

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:33.040
And he was joined by a number
of free thinkers who were drawn

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:35.600
from the realms of medicine,
science and the arts.

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:43.440
Price was really a very
flamboyant character.

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:48.640
A Welsh nationalist, and
self-appointed Archdruid of Wales.

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:58.920
Price was arrested, and sent
to trial at Cardiff assizes

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:03.960
where Judge Stephen ruled
that cremation was not illegal,

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:07.120
providing it didn’t cause
any public nuisance.

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:08.760
So in 1902...

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:25.520
So by the time we get
into the 1940s and 50s,

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:27.800
something really interesting
was happening.

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:30.400
You were having women dying

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:35.560
who no longer had a husband's
grave that they had to go into.

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:39.800
So the issue of choice that cremation
was now, as it were, on the cards

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:43.000
and a possibility, and so about
the middle of the 1960s,

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:48.280
that Britain flips from being burial
majority to cremation majority.

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:51.600
It's really extraordinary that the
percentage of deaths that resulted

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:55.240
in cremation in 1939 stood at 3.5%.

00:02:55.320 --> 00:03:00.080
And now of course the cremation rate
in the UK stands at nearly 80%.

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:10.840
From something like the mid-1970s,
the British started doing something

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:14.080
which many people on the continent
to this day find weird.

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:17.360
They started taking cremated remains
away from the crematorium

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:19.720
and doing their own thing with them.

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:22.600
Just seems weird
to call it an urn to me.

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:23.960
I like to think of it
as a piece of art

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:25.800
that just happens to have
my mum and dad inside it.

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:29.920
This is a piece of my grandmother,
my memorial jewellery

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.080
that I keep with me all the time.

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:37.960
All ashes creations are about
keeping our loved ones close.

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:40.880
Any memorial
is for the people left behind.

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:44.000
It's never for the person,

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:48.200
it's for us to carry on
with that memorial.

00:03:48.280 --> 00:03:51.880
It's allowed the dead to become
part of the furniture of our life.

00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:56.600
My ashes creation is a tattoo

00:03:56.680 --> 00:03:58.760
and in it includes the ashes

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:01.920
of my late partner who passed
away two and a half years ago.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:07.200
A small number of people choose
to have ashes mixed into tattoo ink.

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:10.040
He was my friend
and I also got a tattoo.

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:14.280
And their bodies are literally
mingled together.

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:19.960
As chair of The Cremation Society, I
will naturally choose to be cremated.

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:25.000
I think my daughter will have me
incorporated into a vinyl record

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:28.360
because I love music,
maybe something by the Beastie Boys.

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:32.120
I do not belong
to the tattoo generation.

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:34.880
I don’t think there's enough room
in there to fit me in with them.

